WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:06.990
Next up, let's put some of these material instances into action.

2
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:13.710
So with this exercise, we're going to use a decal in order to animate an explosion.

3
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.710
So it will make more sense as we go on.

4
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.070
So to start off with, I'm just going to hit play to demonstrate what we've got.

5
00:00:22.739 --> 00:00:25.460
It can take a second to render this in.

6
00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:32.450
But if I now click on the world, you can see we have a very, very simple explosion here.

7
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.170
Now, let's make that look a little bit more fancy.

8
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.369
So I'm going to stop that.

9
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.049
Now, we already have our material and our material instance,

10
00:00:44.689 --> 00:00:47.090
and we're going to be using a decal for this.

11
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:52.369
So let's open up our BP underscore targeting.

12
00:00:55.009 --> 00:00:57.009
Drag that in, kill it down.

13
00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:01.810
Now, you're going to see that this has a couple of things in it as part of it.

14
00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.409
We have a decal as part of it, and we have a Niagara system.

15
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:12.180
And what this is going to do is have a nice tight function here.

16
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.459
It just says explode, let's then delay five seconds,

17
00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:18.019
and then destroy the actor that we've just blown on.

18
00:01:18.819 --> 00:01:23.939
But what we want to do is we're going to play around with this decal before.

19
00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:27.219
So in order to do that, what we're going to do

20
00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.700
is we're going to place a little bit of code before this.

21
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.260
We're going to add in a timeline.

22
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.980
So if I drag off of the begin play and type in timeline,

23
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.219
you'll see at the bottom here, we have an option to add timeline.

24
00:01:44.019 --> 00:01:45.379
I'm going to left click on that.

25
00:01:46.019 --> 00:01:49.459
I'm not going to bother renaming it, so I'll just leave it as timeline.

26
00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:53.299
But the one thing that I will do is with a timeline,

27
00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:57.299
a timeline will play for a certain period of time.

28
00:01:57.859 --> 00:02:00.900
And during that, on every frame of the game,

29
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.900
it will execute any of the code which is connected to the update hit.

30
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.050
Now, this is where we're going to be doing the animation.

31
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.370
It's sort of going to be controlling the decal.

32
00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.129
However, we don't want this to explode on every frame.

33
00:02:16.129 --> 00:02:19.250
We only want that to happen after the timeline is finished.

34
00:02:19.729 --> 00:02:21.729
So I'm going to hold down control.

35
00:02:21.729 --> 00:02:26.610
I'm going to drag that string so that it plugs into the explode.

36
00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:30.740
And then we're going to put our code onto the update him.

37
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.430
Right. So now I'm going to double click on the timeline.

38
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.449
And the first thing I'm going to do is change the length of the timeline.

39
00:02:42.449 --> 00:02:45.409
So we can see at the top here, we have five seconds.

40
00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:47.330
Let's change that to three.

41
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.610
So it's a bit faster.

42
00:02:49.949 --> 00:02:52.270
And I'm going to add in a track.

43
00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.629
So I'm going to hit plus, and this is going to give me some options.

44
00:02:55.629 --> 00:03:00.750
We're going to do two tracks in total, but let's start with a float track.

45
00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:05.389
So I'm going to take a float track, and I'm going to call this progress.

46
00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:15.979
Now with this, I'm now going to add in two keys.

47
00:03:15.979 --> 00:03:21.740
So to do that, I will right-hand click on here, and I'll go add a key to float.

48
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.800
I'm going to scroll out, and I'm going to add a key to the curve float at the end.

49
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.759
I'm going to move the end one a little bit below.

50
00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:39.629
And the first one, I'm going to change this so that it is at time zero.

51
00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:41.629
It's going to be value one.

52
00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:45.710
If I push these two, it'll make it fit onto the screen.

53
00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:48.430
So we've effectively got a line that's going down.

54
00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:53.840
Now I will demonstrate this by just showing you the outside.

55
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.039
You'll now see that we have a progress output pin,

56
00:03:57.039 --> 00:04:02.159
and this is going to provide us a float value on every tick update.

57
00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:06.669
And it will be the value of where this curve is.

58
00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:12.349
So if the time was at zero, it would provide the value of one.

59
00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:18.589
If the time was at one second, it would be about 0.6.

60
00:04:19.389 --> 00:04:24.670
If it was at two seconds, it's going to be about 0.2.5, and so on.

61
00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:29.949
So this value will update on frame, and that's what we can use for the animation.

62
00:04:31.279 --> 00:04:35.839
Now I want to do a second track, and for this I'm going to use a color.

63
00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:40.639
So if I come up to add track, I'm going to select add color track.

64
00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.180
And I'm just going to name this color.

65
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.699
Now this looks a little bit different to the other track here.

66
00:04:49.699 --> 00:04:53.540
So you can see that we have a couple of different pins.

67
00:04:54.899 --> 00:04:59.620
These pins can be moved around, and we can even change them.

68
00:05:00.259 --> 00:05:03.779
Now the top pins is going to determine the color,

69
00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.300
and the bottom pins are going to determine the alpha.

70
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.420
And these are usually used the alpha for transparency.

71
00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.360
Now we are only really concerned about the top pins in this,

72
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.279
because we want the color of this decal to change from red to white.

73
00:05:21.899 --> 00:05:27.180
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to drag the white pin over to here.

74
00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:32.509
I'm going to add a key here, double-click.

75
00:05:33.069 --> 00:05:35.550
I'm going to make this entirely red.

76
00:05:36.509 --> 00:05:40.509
So that will change that to entirely red.

77
00:05:41.149 --> 00:05:44.269
I'm going to change the first one to entirely red as well.

78
00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:50.939
So that way we have hit red until about halfway through.

79
00:05:50.939 --> 00:05:55.100
Then it will slowly transition to white, and we'll have white.

80
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:02.319
This gives me a color which will update over time in the same way that our progress bar.

81
00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:10.800
Now I'm going to go to my event graph, and I want to control the decal.

82
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.279
I want to control the material on the decal.

83
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.759
So what I'm going to do is you can see here we have a material instance.

84
00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:22.959
Now it's worth seeing how we set this up,

85
00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:31.089
because we want to set up something that's called a dynamic material instance.

86
00:06:31.089 --> 00:06:33.410
We need to do this on construction script.

87
00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.730
So this is the first point that anything is set.

88
00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.300
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to drag off of the construction script,

89
00:06:41.019 --> 00:06:48.740
and I'm going to type in create dynamic material instance.

90
00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:58.699
And you can see that we have the material here.

91
00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:01.660
So if I was to double-click on the material, the countdown timer,

92
00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:08.339
see that what we've got is we've got a circle which is coming into itself.

93
00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:13.230
If I click on real time, you can see that it's moving here.

94
00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:20.339
And now it's an animated circle that we're using to create the decal.

95
00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:24.449
You can see that this is of the material domain decal.

96
00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:25.250
So this is great.

97
00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.529
We can use this countdown timer and create our material instance.

98
00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.889
And we have a couple of values that we can control.

99
00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:33.329
One of them is progress.

100
00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.769
So you can see that is a parameter here.

101
00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.860
And one of them is color.

102
00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.019
Let's just spot that while we're here.

103
00:07:43.019 --> 00:07:43.579
Here you go.

104
00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.420
Circle color which defaults to red.

105
00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:50.139
Those are the two ones we're going to control.

106
00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:54.050
So we're creating a dynamic material instance.

107
00:07:54.689 --> 00:07:58.850
Now this is interesting because before what we had was a static material instance

108
00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.250
where you had to manually update the asset.

109
00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.410
This is going to create a dynamic material instance just for this.

110
00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.290
Now this isn't particularly performant because you have thousands of assets

111
00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:13.250
which all have dynamic material instances on them.

112
00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:19.810
They're all calculated in separate draw buckets or draw calls.

113
00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.410
So they are a little bit costly.

114
00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:29.569
But if you want to update stuff at runtime, this is probably your best option.

115
00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:34.340
OK, so we have the dynamic material instance.

116
00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:39.779
And what I'm going to do then is I will grab the timer

117
00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.049
and I'm going to plug that in.

118
00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:43.889
So we have a saved variable.

119
00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.769
But the parent, we should also have to set the parent.

120
00:08:48.259 --> 00:08:50.340
And if I search for countdown timer,

121
00:08:51.309 --> 00:08:58.110
this is going to be the parent that we are going to create the dynamic material instance of.

122
00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:03.580
Now what we need to do is we need to apply this.

123
00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:10.500
So if I drag in the decal and I go set decal in material

124
00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:17.360
and plug that in, and then plug that in there.

125
00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.639
That means that we are creating a dynamic material.

126
00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.159
Saving a reference so that we can update the variables on it later.

127
00:09:26.879 --> 00:09:30.480
And then we're applying that material to the decal.

128
00:09:31.889 --> 00:09:35.090
OK, so now we have our material.

129
00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:38.769
Let's use our timeline to update it.

130
00:09:40.029 --> 00:09:41.710
So I'm going back to our event graph.

131
00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.710
And the code that we want to do is on the update section here.

132
00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:52.620
So in order to do that, I'm going to drag in our material reference.

133
00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.059
I'm going to get the timer.

134
00:09:55.179 --> 00:10:00.299
And the first thing I'm going to do is set scalar parameter value.

135
00:10:02.179 --> 00:10:05.460
And I'm going to plug that into the update here.

136
00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:07.970
Now this is going to give me a couple of options.

137
00:10:09.409 --> 00:10:10.610
It wants two inputs.

138
00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:14.450
It wants the value and I can connect the value up to the progress.

139
00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.899
And it wants the name of the parameter.

140
00:10:18.669 --> 00:10:21.309
And so that is progress.

141
00:10:21.309 --> 00:10:24.909
And we can see that here on the material.

142
00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:31.889
Now the next thing I need to do is set the color.

143
00:10:31.889 --> 00:10:42.100
So for this, I'm going to set the vector parameter value and plug that in.

144
00:10:43.039 --> 00:10:44.799
Connect that up to the color here.

145
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:51.460
And the value that we want is circle color.

146
00:10:52.669 --> 00:10:54.190
So there you go, circle color.

147
00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.429
Again, we can check in the material.

148
00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.169
You can see that we have circle color as a parameter here.

149
00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.690
So on tick, it's going to use the timeline

150
00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:11.730
in order to update the progress and the color of the value.

151
00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.929
So if I was to compile and save now, let's test this and see if it works.

152
00:11:18.029 --> 00:11:19.309
So I press play again.

153
00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.330
And now we need to let the shader load.

154
00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:37.490
You can see that we have a circle which is constricting into the center.

155
00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:38.370
And then it's stuck.

156
00:11:38.929 --> 00:11:40.769
So it gives us a kind of effect.

157
00:11:40.769 --> 00:11:47.169
But all of these material is controlled by dynamic instance at runtime.

158
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.799
So that's just an example of one of the most common ways to dynamically use materials.

159
00:11:54.799 --> 00:11:59.519
And it can be used for any of the inputs.

160
00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:09.279
That you have in the material itself.
